I cannot provide a complete web application in C with user authentication directly through this interface. However, I can guide you on how to implement a basic user authentication system in C for a web application.

Here’s a simple example using the **CGI (Common Gateway Interface)** model with C, which handles user login via a form. This example includes:

- A simple login form (HTML).
- A C program that processes the login (acts as a CGI script).
- Basic authentication logic (hardcoded username/password for demonstration).

---

### 1. `login.html` – Simple Login Form

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Login</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h2>Login</h2>
    <form action="/cgi-bin/auth.cgi" method="POST">
        Username: <input type="text" name="username" /><br/>
        Password: <input type="password" name="password" /><br/>
        <input type="submit" value="Login" />
    </form>
</body>
</html>
```

---

### 2. `auth.cgi` – C CGI Program for Authentication

Save this as `auth.c`, then compile it to `auth.cgi`.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

// Simple helper to read POST data
int read_post_data(char *buffer, int max) {
    char *content_len_str = getenv("CONTENT_LENGTH");
    int content_len = 0;

    if (content_len_str != NULL) {
        content_len = atoi(content_len_str);
    }

    if (content_len >= max) content_len = max - 1;
    int i = 0;
    while (i < content_len) {
        int c = fgetc(stdin);
        if (c == EOF) break;
        buffer[i++] = (char)c;
    }
    buffer[i] = '\0';
    return i;
}

// Parse form data (very basic URL-decoding)
void parse_form_data(char *data, char *username, char *password) {
    char *pair;
    char *saveptr;

    // Split on '&'
    pair = strtok_r(data, "&", &saveptr);
    while (pair != NULL) {
        char *key = strtok(pair, "=");
        char *value = strtok(NULL, "=");
        if (value == NULL) value = "";

        // Simple URL decode (replace + with space, handle %XX later if needed)
        char *decoded_value = value;
        for (int i = 0; decoded_value[i]; i++) {
            if (decoded_value[i] == '+') decoded_value[i] = ' ';
        }

        if (strcmp(key, "username") == 0) {
            strcpy(username, decoded_value);
        } else if (strcmp(key, "password") == 0) {
            strcpy(password, decoded_value);
        }
        pair = strtok_r(NULL, "&", &saveptr);
    }
}

int main() {
    char post_data[1024];
    char username[64] = "";
    char password[64] = "";

    printf("Content-Type: text/html\n\n");

    // Read POST data
    read_post_data(post_data, sizeof(post_data));

    // Parse form fields
    parse_form_data(post_data, username, password);

    // Hardcoded credentials (in real app: use hashed passwords & database)
    if (strcmp(username, "admin") == 0 && strcmp(password, "secret") == 0) {
        printf("<h1>Login Successful!</h1>");
        printf("<p>Welcome, %s!</p>", username);
    } else {
        printf("<h1>Login Failed</h1>");
        printf("<p>Invalid username or password.</p>");
        printf("<a href='login.html'>Try again</a>");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

---

### 3. Compile and Set Up

On a Linux server with CGI support:

```bash
gcc -o auth.cgi auth.c
chmod +x auth.cgi
mv auth.cgi /usr/lib/cgi-bin/  # or your server's CGI directory
```

Place `login.html` in your web server's document root (e.g., `/var/www/html/`).

---

### Notes:
- This is **not secure** for production (no password hashing, no SQL, no CSRF protection).
- For production, consider using:
  - A web framework in C (like **Mongoose**, **libonion**, or **G-WAN**).
  - Secure password hashing (e.g., **bcrypt** via a C library).
  - Session management with tokens.
  - HTTPS and secure headers.

Would you like me to generate a version using **Mongoose** (a lightweight web server library in C) for a more robust example?

